2. Preliminaries

2.1 Environment and Agent

We consider an agent interacting with an environment modeled as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) with state space \mathcal{X}, action space \mathcal{A}, transition kernel P^e(x' \mid x, a), reward function R^e(x, a), and discount factor \gamma \in [0,1). The agent has an internal cognitive state s \in \mathcal{S}, where \mathcal{S} is a Polish space. The internal state encodes all information the agent possesses: current belief about the external state, retrieved memories, reasoning context, etc.

The agent can choose external actions a \in \mathcal{A}, or it can perform internal cognitive operations before acting. An external action yields immediate reward and transitions the external state; an internal operation transforms the internal state without directly affecting the external world.

2.2 Cognitive Operations

A cognitive operation o is a (possibly stochastic) measurable mapping T_o : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}. Applying operation o in internal state s produces a new internal state s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s). Each operation incurs a non‑negative cost c(s, o) \geq 0, representing computational resources (time, energy, memory). The set of all primitive operations available to the agent at birth is denoted \mathcal{O}_0; we assume \mathcal{O}_0 is finite.

2.3 Optimal Value Function and Terminal Utility

The optimal external action‑value function, assuming fully rational external behavior from internal state s, is

V^*(s) = \max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t R^e(X_t, A_t) \,\Big|\, S_0 = s, \pi \right],

where the maximization is over policies that map internal states to external actions and cognitive operation choices. The terminal utility of stopping cognitive processing and acting immediately is

U(s) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}[R^e(x, a) \mid s],

with the expectation taken over the agent’s current belief about the external state x encoded in s.

2.4 Future Value (FV)

For any operation o \in \mathcal{O}, its Future Value from state s is defined as

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s)}[V^*(s')] - U(s) - c(s, o). \tag{1}

An operation is worth executing iff \mathrm{FV}(o; s) > 0. The optimal meta‑policy selects an operation with maximal positive FV; if none has positive FV, the agent stops and acts externally.

