3. The Universal Cognitive Bellman Theorem

We now prove that the FV principle is not merely a plausible heuristic but a necessary consequence of minimal rationality axioms for internal computation. Consider an agent that maintains a preference ordering \succsim_s over internal state transitions (i.e., pairs (s, o)). The interpretation is that (s, o_1) \succsim_s (s, o_2) means the agent considers applying operation o_1 in state s at least as desirable as applying o_2. We also allow the option “stop and act” denoted \bot. The agent’s choices are guided by a value function \mathcal{V}(o; s) representing the desirability of o from s.

Axiom 1 (Value Consistency). There exists a continuous, bounded function \mathcal{V} : \mathcal{O} \times \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R} such that for all s, (s, o_1) \succsim_s (s, o_2) iff \mathcal{V}(o_1; s) \ge \mathcal{V}(o_2; s), and \mathcal{V}(\bot; s) = 0.

Axiom 2 (Cost Additivity). If an operation o can be decomposed as the sequential application of o_1 then o_2 (i.e., the state transition satisfies T_o(s' \mid s) = \int T_{o_2}(s' \mid s'') T_{o_1}(s'' \mid s) ds''), then

c(s, o) = c(s, o_1) + \mathbb{E}_{s'' \sim T_{o_1}(\cdot \mid s)}[c(s'', o_2)].

Axiom 3 (Temporal Consistency). For any operation o that is equivalent to doing o_1 followed by a second operation o_2 whose choice may depend on the intermediate state, the desirability satisfies the Bellman‑type recursion:

\mathcal{V}(o; s) = \mathcal{V}(o_1; s) + \mathbb{E}_{s'' \sim T_{o_1}(\cdot \mid s)}[ \max\{ \mathcal{V}(o_2; s''), 0 \} ].

Axiom 4 (Minimality). The agent never performs an operation that leads to a state from which stopping would be strictly worse than stopping immediately, net of costs. Formally, if an operation o always results in a state s' with V^*(s') - U(s') \le c(s, o), then \mathcal{V}(o; s) \le 0.

Theorem 1 (Universal Cognitive Bellman Theorem).
Under Axioms 1–4, the desirability function \mathcal{V} is necessarily of the form

\mathcal{V}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - U(s) - c(s, o) = \mathrm{FV}(o; s),

where V^* is the optimal external value function and U(s) = \max_a \mathbb{E}[R^e(x,a) \mid s].

Proof.
By Axiom 1, we can work directly with \mathcal{V}. For any operation o, consider a hypothetical composite: first apply o, then from the resulting state, act optimally in the external MDP. By Axiom 3 applied with o_1 = o and o_2 being the “optimal external policy” treated as a (possibly stochastic) operation that maps s' to the state resulting from acting externally, we obtain

\mathcal{V}(\text{composite}; s) = \mathcal{V}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s)}[ \max\{ \mathcal{V}(\text{external optimal}; s'), 0\} ].

But external optimality means that from s' the agent can achieve expected discounted return V^*(s'). Stopping and acting externally from s' yields utility U(s') and incurs no further internal cost, so by Axioms 2 and 4, the desirability of “stop and act” is V^*(s') - U(s') (since the external sequence is already accounted in V^*). Therefore \mathcal{V}(\text{external optimal}; s') = V^*(s') - U(s').
Moreover, the composite operation (do o, then act externally) is just a way of acting externally after one step of internal processing. Its desirability must equal V^*(s) - U(s) - c(s,o) because from s, the agent can either stop and get U(s) immediately or perform o and then expect V^*(s') net of the cost. By Axiom 3, that total value is \mathcal{V}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}[\max\{V^*(s') - U(s'), 0\}]. But by the principle of optimality, the value of acting after o is exactly \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')], because the agent can always choose to stop if that is better; hence \mathbb{E}[\max\{V^*(s') - U(s'), 0\}] = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] - U(s') (since V^*(s') \ge U(s') always holds, as acting can at least achieve the immediate action value). Substituting,

V^*(s) - U(s) - c(s,o) = \mathcal{V}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] - U(s'),

and because U(s') = \mathbb{E}[R^e] while the transition from s to s' does not change the external state, U(s') = U(s) (the immediate action value is constant under pure internal operations). Thus we obtain

\mathcal{V}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] - U(s) - c(s,o).

This is exactly FV. ∎

This theorem establishes FV as the only consistent desirability measure for cognitive operations under the stated rationality axioms. It thus provides a normative foundation: any bounded‑rational agent must use FV to decide whether and which internal operations to execute.

