4. Derivation of the Composition Operator

Instead of assuming a composition operator \mathcal{C}, we now derive it from the requirement that composed operations must be realizable as sequential state transitions. Let \mathcal{O} be the set of all operations for which an FV is defined. For any finite sequence o_1, \dots, o_k \in \mathcal{O}, the concatenated state transition is

T_{o_1 \circ \dots \circ o_k}(s' \mid s) = \int \cdots \int T_{o_k}(s' \mid s_{k-1}) \cdots T_{o_1}(s_1 \mid s) \, ds_1 \cdots ds_{k-1}.

We define the composition of these operations as the operation o whose transition kernel is precisely this concatenation, and whose cost is given by Axiom 2 recursively:

c(s, o) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\big[ c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s \big],

where S_0 = s and S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}).

Definition 1 (Composition Operator). For any k \ge 1, define

\mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_k) = \text{the operation with transition } T_{o_1 \circ \dots \circ o_k} \text{ and cost as above}.

Lemma 1 (Closure under sequential composition). If each o_i belongs to a set \mathcal{O} that is closed under the transition‑kernel combination (e.g., all finite‑mixture of primitive kernels), then \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_k) \in \mathcal{O}.

Proof. Follows from the closure properties of the set of Markov kernels on \mathcal{S} under sequential composition. ∎

Lemma 2 (Associativity). \mathcal{C}\big( \mathcal{C}(o_1, o_2), o_3 \big) = \mathcal{C}(o_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2, o_3)).

Proof. Both sides correspond to the three‑step transition T_{o_1} \circ T_{o_2} \circ T_{o_3} with total cost \mathbb{E}[c(s, o_1) + c(S_1, o_2) + c(S_2, o_3)], which is independent of the grouping. ∎

Lemma 3 (Cost Additivity). For the composed operation o = \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_k), the cost satisfies

c(s, o) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\big[ c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s \big].

Proof. By construction. ∎

Theorem 2 (FV of Composed Operations). For o = \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_k), the Future Value from state s is

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\big[ \mathrm{FV}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \mid s \big],

provided the FV recursion (1) holds for each component.

Proof. Expanding the definition,

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_k) \mid s] - U(s) - \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s].

Write U(s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_0)] - \mathrm{FV}(o_1; s) - c(s, o_1) - \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_1)] + \dots telescopically. The intermediate V^* terms cancel, leaving the stated sum. ∎

Thus the composition operator preserves the FV structure: the FV of a sequence is the expected sum of the FVs of its steps, exactly as intuition would suggest. This is crucial for efficient evaluation and learning.

