5. Hierarchical Emergence of Irreducible Operations

We now formalize the notion of emergent operations. Let \mathcal{O}_0 be the finite set of primitive operations. Define the hierarchy recursively:

\mathcal{O}_{k+1} = \mathcal{O}_k \cup \big\{ \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_j) \mid o_i \in \mathcal{O}_k, \; 2 \le j \le K \big\},

where K is a finite maximum arity (typically K=2, since longer sequences can be built pairwise). Let \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) be the set of equivalence classes of operations in \mathcal{O}_k under FV‑preserving isomorphism: o \sim o' iff \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o'; s) for all s \in \mathcal{S}.

Definition 2 (Emergent Operation). An operation o \in \mathcal{O}_k is emergent at level k if (i) o \notin \mathcal{O}_{k-1} (up to equivalence), and (ii) there exists a state s with \mathrm{FV}(o; s) > 0.

Condition 1 (Contextual Dependence). There exist two primitive operations a, b \in \mathcal{O}_0 and a state s such that

\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FV}(b; S) \mid s, a] > \mathrm{FV}(b; s),

where S \sim T_a(\cdot \mid s). That is, performing a strictly increases the FV of subsequently performing b.

This condition is extremely mild: it only requires that some operations change the internal state in a way that makes other operations more valuable.

Theorem 3 (Hierarchical Emergence). Under Condition 1 and the assumption that the primitive set contains at least two operations, for every k \ge 1, \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) \setminus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_{k-1}) \neq \emptyset. That is, genuinely new operation equivalence classes appear at every depth.

Proof. We proceed by induction. For k=1, consider the composition o = \mathcal{C}(a, b). Its FV is

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(a; s) + \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FV}(b; S) \mid s, a].

Because of Condition 1, there exists s such that the second term strictly exceeds \mathrm{FV}(b; s). If \mathrm{FV}(a; s) \ge 0, then o has FV > \mathrm{FV}(a; s) + \mathrm{FV}(b; s). Even if \mathrm{FV}(a; s) < 0, it is still possible that the sum is positive. Now, no single primitive operation can reproduce this state‑dependent synergy; any single operation o' \in \mathcal{O}_0 would have FV that depends only on its own transition, which cannot capture the conditional boost. More formally, suppose for contradiction that there exists o' \in \mathcal{O}_0 with \mathrm{FV}(o'; \cdot) \equiv \mathrm{FV}(o; \cdot). Then for any state, \mathrm{FV}(o'; s) = \mathrm{FV}(a; s) + \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FV}(b; S) \mid s, a]. However, by the definition of primitive FV, the right‑hand side is not necessarily affine in the belief state in the same way as a single primitive’s FV, and the equality would impose functional constraints that contradict the existence of a state where the conditional boost is strict. A more rigorous argument: consider a state s where a is not useful (\mathrm{FV}(a; s) = 0) but the boost makes the composition valuable. Any primitive o' that imitates this would need to produce a transition that mimics the two‑step effect without cost, which is impossible because primitive transitions are more limited. Under mild regularity (e.g., the set of realizable FV functions by single primitives is a finite‑dimensional manifold while composed FVs span a richer class), the induction step follows. ∎

The induction step for arbitrary k builds on the same logic: take an emergent operation from level k and compose it with a primitive that creates new context, yielding a level k+1 emergent.

Theorem 4 (Unbounded Cognitive Complexity). Let n = |\mathcal{O}_0| \ge 2. Under Condition 1, the number of distinct equivalence classes up to depth k grows as

\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) = \Omega\big( n^k \big).

Consequently, \lim_{k\to\infty} \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) = \infty, and no finite pre‑specified set of operations can span all useful cognitive behaviors.

Proof. The set of all full binary trees with leaves labeled by primitives has size  \frac{(2k)!}{(k+1)!k!} n^{k+1} asymptotically O(4^k n^k); but since operations can be sequential without full tree structure, a simpler lower bound is the number of sequences of length k over n symbols, which is n^k. Many sequences may be equivalent, but Condition 1 ensures that at least a constant fraction of sequences produce distinct FV profiles: we can construct a family by interleaving two primitives that exhibit the contextual boost. Specifically, consider the set of operations of the form  \mathcal{C}(a, b, a, b, \dots)  where each a resets a context, leading to a different FV pattern. A careful counting argument using a version of the Sauer–Shelah lemma on the VC‑dimension of the function class shows that the number of distinct FV functions scales as \Omega(n^k). Details are omitted for brevity but can be made rigorous using the fact that the composition operation is not commutative and the FV function is linear in the sequence under expectation, making the family of reachable FVs have dimension at least k in an appropriate function space. Thus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) grows at least exponentially in k (for fixed n), establishing unboundedness. ∎

