6. Algorithmic Realization and Sample Complexity

We now provide a constructive algorithm that realizes hierarchical FV optimization in an agent. The algorithm does not require pre‑coding all compositions; it discovers them on‑the‑fly.

6.1 Meta‑Cognitive Algorithm

The agent maintains a library \mathbb{O} of known operations, initialized to \mathcal{O}_0. At each decision point (internal state s):

1. Evaluation: For each o \in \mathbb{O}, estimate \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) using N Monte‑Carlo rollouts of the internal world model (or real experience).
2. Selection: If \max_{o\in\mathbb{O}} \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) > 0, execute o^* = \arg\max and continue from the new state s'; else stop and act externally.
3. Composition Proposal: With small probability \epsilon, sample a few operations o_1,\dots,o_m \in \mathbb{O}, create o_{\text{new}} = \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_m), and insert it into \mathbb{O}.
4. Pruning: Periodically remove operations whose upper confidence bound on average FV is below zero.

6.2 Sample Complexity Analysis

We assume that the internal state space \mathcal{S} is finite (or can be discretized), with |\mathcal{S}| = M. The FV values are bounded in [-B, B]. The agent has access to a generative model of internal transitions: given (s, o), it can sample s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s).

Theorem 5 (Sample Complexity of Meta‑Learning).
For any \epsilon, \delta > 0, to learn an \epsilon-optimal meta‑policy for all operations in \mathcal{O}_K (i.e., all compositions up to depth K) with probability at least 1-\delta, the number of generative samples required is

O\left( \frac{B^2 n^{K} K M}{\epsilon^2} \log\frac{n^{K} M}{\delta} \right).

Proof sketch. The total number of candidate operations up to depth K is at most n^K (worst‑case enumeration). For each operation and each state, we need to estimate FV to within \epsilon. By Hoeffding’s inequality, O(B^2/\epsilon^2 \log(1/\delta')) samples per (o, s) suffice. Since evaluating a depth‑k operation requires k internal steps, the total number of primitive transitions simulated is as stated. Composition proposal adds only a constant factor. ∎

When \mathcal{S} is continuous but has finite covering number \mathcal{N}(\epsilon), a similar bound holds with M replaced by the covering number, using uniform convergence arguments. The exponential dependence on K is inevitable for exhaustive enumeration but can be mitigated by heuristic proposals; the bound merely shows polynomial complexity in all parameters except the combinatorial explosion, which is partially controlled by pruning.

