7. Unification of Perception, Reasoning, and Memory

We now demonstrate that the three previously separate theories are exact instances of FV.

7.1 Perception as Decision‑Centric Rate‑Distortion

A perception operation o_{\text{perc}} corresponds to acquiring an observation y with associated encoding cost. The internal state is updated to s' = s \oplus (y, \text{encoding}). The cost is c(s, o_{\text{perc}}) = \lambda \cdot I(X; Y \mid s). Then

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \mathbb{E}_{y}[V^*(s \oplus y)] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y|s),

exactly the decision‑centric rate‑distortion objective. The FV‑optimal policy decides when to acquire a new observation.

7.2 Reasoning as Optimal Stopping with FRV

A reasoning step o_{\text{reason}} refines the internal state via a chain‑of‑thought. Its cost is the token or FLOP count. The FV reduces to the Future Reasoning Value:

\mathrm{FRV}(o_{\text{reason}}; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] - U(s) - c(s, o_{\text{reason}}),

and the optimal rule is to stop when \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0.

7.3 Memory Admission with FMV

A memory operation o_{\text{mem}} stores a tuple m into an external memory. The cost is storage overhead. Then

\mathrm{FMV}(o_{\text{mem}}; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}},

and only memories with positive FMV are stored.

7.4 Cross‑Modal Composition

Because all operations share the same FV metric, cross‑modal compositions like perception → reasoning, reasoning → memory, etc., are evaluated by the same telescoping sum. The contextual dependence condition holds universally: for example, an observation often increases the value of a subsequent reasoning step, making the composed operation emergent and strictly more valuable than either alone. The agent thus automatically learns to sequence perception and reasoning in an optimal order without any hard‑coded pipeline.

