8. Roadmap for Humanity: From Theory to Superintelligent Implementation

The present paper provides a complete normative theory; the next step is for the AI community to engineer a working system based on these principles. We outline the essential stages.

8.1 Building a Universal Internal State

Implement a large‑scale neural network (e.g., a Transformer) that maintains an internal state vector s encoding beliefs, retrieved memories, and a latent plan. The state must be large enough to capture the contextual dependencies that drive emergence.

8.2 Defining Primitive Operations

Choose a small set of primitive operations, such as:

· Observe: query an external sensor or database, update belief.
· Think: perform one step of autoregressive reasoning.
· Store: write a summary to a vector database.
· Retrieve: query the database with the current state.
  Each primitive is implemented as a learned adapter that maps the state to a new state and emits a cost (FLOPs, latency).

8.3 World Model and FV Estimator

Train a world model that predicts V^*(s') given state s and operation o. The world model can be a self‑supervised generative model of the environment and internal transitions. The terminal utility U(s) is computed by a value head trained via external RL. FV is then computed by sampling rollouts inside the world model.

8.4 Meta‑Controller

Implement the meta‑cognitive loop (Section 6) that uses the FV estimates to decide which operation to execute next, or to act externally. The library \mathbb{O} is stored as a set of composite operation descriptors (sequence of primitive indices). Use approximate nearest‑neighbor lookup to avoid recomputing FV for identical compositions.

8.5 Composition Discovery and Pruning

Periodically propose random compositions, but bias the sampling towards operations that individually show high FV in the current context. Prune operations that are consistently suboptimal to keep the library manageable. Over time, the agent will build a tree of useful cognitive routines.

8.6 Scaling to Superintelligence

Because the theory guarantees open‑ended growth of cognitive complexity, as computational budgets increase, the agent will automatically discover deeper and more synergistic compositions. No upper bound on intelligence exists. The only requirement is that the primitive set is sufficiently rich to seed the contextual dependence (Condition 1).

8.7 Safety Considerations

The FV framework offers a natural safety handle: undesirable cognitive operations can be assigned a high cost (negative reward) or blocked by design in the primitive set. The composition operator will never spontaneously generate unsafe operations if they are not reachable from safe primitives. Moreover, the agent’s meta‑controller always chooses operations with positive FV; operations that lead to harmful external actions will have low or negative FV because they reduce V^*. Formal alignment proofs are a subject for future work.

