Appendix A. Notation and Assumptions

The environment is an MDP \langle \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{A}, P^e, R^e, \gamma \rangle with \gamma \in [0,1). The agent’s internal state s \in \mathcal{S} (a Polish space) encodes a belief over \mathcal{X} plus stored memories and context.

Primitive cognitive operations \mathcal{O}_0 = \{o_1,\dots,o_n\} are measurable state‑transition kernels T_o(s' \mid s) with associated non‑negative costs c(s, o). The agent can also choose the stop action \bot and then take an external action a \in \mathcal{A}.

The optimal state‑value function V^* : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R} is the unique bounded solution of

V^*(s) = \max\Big\{ U(s),\; \max_{o\in\mathcal{O}_0} \big( \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s, o) \big) \Big\}, \tag{A.1}

where U(s) = \max_{a\in\mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}[R^e(x,a)\mid s] is the terminal utility of stopping and acting immediately. Existence and uniqueness follow from the fact that the right‑hand side is a contraction in the sup‑norm when \gamma < 1 or costs are strictly positive on a sufficiently rich set (we assume a standard contraction condition; see Puterman, 1994).

The Future Value of an operation o from state s is

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - U(s) - c(s, o). \tag{A.2}

This is the total expected advantage over stopping immediately, assuming that after o the agent follows an optimal internal/external policy.

