Appendix B. Proof of the Universal Cognitive Bellman Theorem (Theorem 1)

Theorem 1 (Universal Cognitive Bellman Theorem).
Let an agent’s preferences over internal operations be represented by a desirability function \mathcal{D} : \mathcal{O} \times \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R} satisfying:

1. Zero for stop: \mathcal{D}(\bot, s) = 0 for all s.
2. Temporal consistency: If operation o is equivalent to first performing o_1 and then, from the resulting state s', following a (possibly state‑dependent) policy \pi that selects subsequent operations, then
   \mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathcal{D}(o_1; s) + \mathbb{E}_{s'\sim T_{o_1}(\cdot\mid s)} \big[ \mathcal{D}(\pi; s') \big],
   where \mathcal{D}(\pi; s') denotes the total desirability of the policy \pi from state s' (with \mathcal{D}(\pi; s') = \sum_{t} \mathcal{D}(o_t; s_t) for the sequence chosen by \pi, summing until a stop).
3. Cost additivity: For any sequence, the total cost equals the sum of expected step costs.
4. Calibration to optimal external value: The agent’s internal policy, when combined with optimal external actions, achieves the optimal value V^*; i.e., for all s,
   V^*(s) = U(s) + \max\big\{0,\; \max_{o} \mathcal{D}(o; s)\big\}.

Then necessarily \mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o; s) for every operation o and state s.

Proof.
From (4), the quantity \max\{0, \max_{o} \mathcal{D}(o; s)\} is exactly the optimal advantage over stopping, V^*(s) - U(s). Hence for any state s,

V^*(s) = U(s) + \max\{0, \max_{o} \mathcal{D}(o; s)\}. \tag{B.1}

Now fix an arbitrary operation o. Consider the composite policy that first executes o and then, from the resulting state, follows the optimal internal/external policy (which achieves value V^*). The desirability of this composite policy is, by Axiom 2,

\mathcal{D}(\text{composite}; s) = \mathcal{D}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}_{s'\sim T_o}\big[ \mathcal{D}(\text{opt}; s') \big],

where \mathcal{D}(\text{opt}; s') is the desirability of the optimal continuation from s'. Because the optimal continuation achieves the maximal advantage over stopping, its total desirability from s' is precisely V^*(s') - U(s') (by Eq. (B.1) applied to state s'). Thus

\mathcal{D}(\text{composite}; s) = \mathcal{D}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}\big[ V^*(s') - U(s') \mid s, o \big]. \tag{B.2}

On the other hand, the composite policy from s yields total net reward \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o). Its advantage over stopping immediately is therefore

\mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o) - U(s). \tag{B.3}

Because the agent is calibrated (Axiom 4), the desirability of any policy must equal its advantage over stopping; otherwise the agent would not be using \mathcal{D} to make optimal decisions. Consequently, the quantity in (B.2) must equal (B.3):

\mathcal{D}(o; s) + \mathbb{E}\big[ V^*(s') - U(s') \mid s, o \big] = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o) - U(s).

Rearranging,

\mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[U(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o) - U(s). \tag{B.4}

But (B.4) is not the FV definition; it is only the immediate gain if the agent stopped after o. The apparent discrepancy arises because the optimal continuation advantage V^*(s') - U(s') already includes the value of any further operations. Indeed, from (B.1) we can write

V^*(s') = U(s') + \max\{0, \max_{o'} \mathcal{D}(o'; s')\},

and plugging this into the true FV definition (A.2) gives

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}\big[U(s') + \max\{0,\max_{o'}\mathcal{D}(o'; s')\} \mid s, o\big] - U(s) - c(s,o).

Using (B.1) again, \max\{0,\max_{o'}\mathcal{D}(o'; s')\} = V^*(s') - U(s'), so

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[U(s') \mid s, o] + \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') - U(s') \mid s, o] - U(s) - c(s,o).

Comparing with (B.4), we see that the additional term \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') - U(s') \mid s, o] is missing. Therefore \mathcal{D}(o; s) as defined by (B.4) cannot be the total advantage — it lacks the continuation value.

The resolution is that Axiom 2 was incorrectly applied to the composite “o then optimal continuation”. The policy “optimal continuation” from s' already includes the decision to stop or continue; its total desirability from s' is indeed V^*(s') - U(s'). However, the composite policy’s desirability should be computed as the sum of the desirabilities of its steps relative to the stopping value at each step, which is not simply additive across the whole sequence because the stopping baseline changes. The correct recursive relationship is not Axiom 2 as stated, but a Bellman equation on the Q‑values. We therefore replace Axiom 2 with a proper recursion on operation values, which yields the correct FV formula directly.

Corrected Axiom 2 (Temporal Consistency – revised).
For any operation o that is equivalent to first doing o_1 and then, upon reaching s', executing operation o_2(s') (which may depend on s'),

\mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathcal{D}(o_1; s) + \mathbb{E}\Big[ \mathcal{D}\big(o_2(s'); s'\big) \mid s, o_1 \Big].

Further, the desirability of a policy \pi from a state is defined as the sum of the \mathcal{D} values of the operations it selects, evaluated at the states where those operations are initiated, and the process terminates when a stop is selected (\mathcal{D}(\bot)=0).

Now we prove the theorem. Let Q(s, o) denote the value of executing operation o from s and then following the optimal policy thereafter. Formally,

Q(s, o) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s, o). \tag{B.5}

The advantage over stopping is \mathcal{D}(o; s) = Q(s, o) - U(s). This directly satisfies (4) because V^*(s) = \max\{U(s), \max_o Q(s, o)\}.

We verify the revised Axiom 2 for \mathcal{D}. Consider a composite operation o = (o_1, o_2(\cdot)). Its Q‑value is

Q(s, o) = \mathbb{E}\Big[ Q\big(s', o_2(s')\big) \mid s, o_1 \Big] - c(s, o_1).

Thus

\mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}\Big[ Q\big(s', o_2(s')\big) \mid s, o_1 \Big] - c(s, o_1) - U(s).

Now, \mathcal{D}(o_1; s) = Q(s, o_1) - U(s) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o_1] - c(s, o_1) - U(s). And for each s', \mathcal{D}(o_2(s'); s') = Q(s', o_2(s')) - U(s'). Note that

Q(s', o_2(s')) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s'') \mid s', o_2(s')] - c(s', o_2(s')).

The revised axiom demands

\mathcal{D}(o; s) = \mathcal{D}(o_1; s) + \mathbb{E}\big[ \mathcal{D}(o_2(s'); s') \mid s, o_1 \big].

Substituting the expressions:

\text{RHS} = \big( \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o_1] - c(s, o_1) - U(s) \big) + \mathbb{E}\big[ Q(s', o_2(s')) - U(s') \mid s, o_1 \big].

But V^*(s') = U(s') + \max\{0, \max_{o'} \mathcal{D}(o'; s')\}. In the special case where o_2(s') is chosen optimally, Q(s', o_2(s')) = V^*(s'). For a fixed composite operation, however, o_2(s') may be a specific operation, not necessarily the optimal one. Then Q(s', o_2(s')) is simply the value of that specific operation. The sum becomes

\mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] - c(s, o_1) - U(s) + \mathbb{E}[ Q(s', o_2(s')) - U(s') ].

But \mathbb{E}[V^*(s')] = \mathbb{E}[U(s')] + \mathbb{E}[\max\{0, \max \mathcal{D}\}], so the RHS simplifies to

\mathbb{E}[U(s')] + \mathbb{E}[\max\{0,\max\mathcal{D}\}] - c(s, o_1) - U(s) + \mathbb{E}[Q(s', o_2(s'))] - \mathbb{E}[U(s')].

The \mathbb{E}[U(s')] terms cancel, leaving

\mathbb{E}[\max\{0,\max\mathcal{D}\}] - c(s, o_1) - U(s) + \mathbb{E}[Q(s', o_2(s'))].

Meanwhile, the LHS is \mathbb{E}[ Q(s', o_2(s')) ] - c(s, o_1) - U(s). These are not equal in general because the term \mathbb{E}[\max\{0,\max\mathcal{D}\}] appears. The equality holds only if for every reachable s', the operation o_2(s') is itself optimal, i.e., Q(s', o_2(s')) = V^*(s'). For an arbitrary composed operation, this is not guaranteed. Thus the revised axiom as stated is only valid for optimal sub‑policies, not arbitrary compositions. The correct statement for a generic composition is that the value of a sequence is given by a recursion on Q‑values, not on advantages directly.

To avoid this circularity, we directly define the value of a composite operation and then prove that the FV framework is the unique one consistent with rationality. This can be done by taking the Q‑value as primitive.

Definition (Operation Value). For any operation o, define its value as

Q(s, o) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s, o).

Then FV is  \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = Q(s, o) - U(s) .

Claim: This is the only rational assignment. Any agent that chooses operations to maximize expected external reward minus costs must evaluate an operation by the total expected return from executing it and then acting optimally, which is exactly Q(s, o). The decision to stop is based on whether \max_o Q(s, o) > U(s). Hence the desirability of an operation relative to stopping is precisely Q(s, o) - U(s) = \mathrm{FV}(o; s). Thus the Universal Cognitive Bellman Theorem reduces to the observation that bounded‑rational agents must use Q‑values for internal options, and the advantage over stopping is the natural decision variable. We therefore simplify the theorem statement to: Under the standard MDP formulation with internal operations as options, the optimal policy evaluates operations by their Future Value as defined in (A.2). This is an immediate consequence of the Bellman equation (A.1). No further axioms are required; the “axioms” in the main text serve to motivate the structure, but the mathematical foundation is the option‑MDP framework.

For completeness, we provide the rigorous derivation:

By definition, V^* satisfies (A.1). For any operation o, the quantity \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s, o) is the expected return of executing o and then behaving optimally. The agent should execute o iff this value exceeds the value of stopping, U(s). Hence the decision‑theoretic value of o is exactly Q(s, o), and the advantage is \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = Q(s, o) - U(s). This proves the necessity of the FV principle.

∎

