Appendix C. Recursive Composition and Value of Sequences

Definition C.1 (Sequential Composition). Given a finite sequence of operations \mathbf{o} = (o_1, o_2, \dots, o_k), the composed operation \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}) has transition kernel

T_{\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})}(s_k \mid s_0) = \int \cdots \int \prod_{i=1}^k T_{o_i}(s_i \mid s_{i-1}) \, ds_1\cdots ds_{k-1},
\]  

and cost

c(s_0, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\big[ c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s_0 \big],
\]  

where S_0 = s_0 and S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}).

Proposition C.1 (Q‑value of a sequence). For \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k),

Q(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \mathbb{E}\Big[ Q\big(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)\big) \mid s, o_1 \Big] - c(s, o_1). \tag{C.1}

Proof. By definition, Q(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_k) \mid s, o_1,\dots,o_k] - \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[c(S_{i-1}, o_i)]. The inner expectation can be written as \mathbb{E}\big[ \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_k) \mid S_1, o_2,\dots,o_k] \mid s, o_1 \big]. But \mathbb{E}[V^*(S_k) \mid S_1, o_2,\dots,o_k] - \sum_{i=2}^k c(S_{i-1}, o_i) = Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) by definition. Hence the total is \mathbb{E}[ Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) + \text{ later costs?} ] Actually the cost for the tail sequence is already included in Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) (since Q(S_1, \cdot) subtracts its own costs). So the total is \mathbb{E}[ Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) \mid s, o_1 ] - c(s, o_1). ∎

Corollary C.2 (FV recursion). The Future Value of a sequence satisfies

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = \mathbb{E}\big[ \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k); S_1) \mid s, o_1 \big] + \mathbb{E}[U(S_1) \mid s, o_1] - U(s) - c(s, o_1). \tag{C.2}

Proof. From Q = \mathrm{FV} + U, we have \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = Q(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) - U(s). Substitute (C.1): = \mathbb{E}[ Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) \mid s, o_1 ] - c(s, o_1) - U(s). Now Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) = \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k); S_1) + U(S_1). Plugging in yields the formula. ∎

This recursion replaces the incorrect simple sum. It shows that the FV of a sequence is not merely the sum of individual FVs; it contains an extra term \mathbb{E}[U(S_1)] - U(s) reflecting the change in stopping value.

