Appendix E. Unbounded Cognitive Complexity (Theorem 4)

Theorem E.1 (Unbounded Complexity). Let n = |\mathcal{O}_0| \ge 2 and assume Condition D.1 holds. Then the number of distinct FV equivalence classes in \mathcal{O}_k grows as \Omega(n^{k}). Consequently, no finite set of operations can span all useful cognitive behaviors.

Proof. We construct a family of sequences of length k from two primitives a,b such that their FV functions are all distinct. By Condition D.1, there exists a state s_0 where the composition ab yields a strict FV improvement over both a and b. Define for a binary string \mathbf{w} \in \{0,1\}^k the operation \mathbf{o}_{\mathbf{w}} as the sequence where we apply a if the bit is 0 and b if 1. We claim that for two different strings \mathbf{w} \neq \mathbf{w}', the FV profiles differ on some state.

Consider the state s_0. Evaluate \mathrm{FV}(\mathbf{o}_{\mathbf{w}}; s_0) using the recursion (C.2) repeatedly. The expression expands into a sum of expected U differences and FVs of tails. Because the effect of a versus b on the stopping value U differs (otherwise they would be equivalent), the cumulative effect of the sequence will depend on the exact pattern. More formally, we can linearize the FV as a function of the sequence’s “path” by assuming the MDP and internal transitions satisfy a linear identifiability condition: the functions s \mapsto U(s), and the transition operators are such that the mapping from sequence to the function s \mapsto \mathrm{FV}(\mathbf{o}_{\mathbf{w}}; s) is injective. This can be achieved if the state space contains a distinct “context” variable that records the history, which is typical in cognitive architectures (the state includes a memory of past operations). In such a setting, each distinct sequence produces a different final state distribution and thus a different FV on a fixed set of probe states. A counting argument then gives at least 2^k distinct classes for n=2. For general n, we can use n primitives and get \Omega(n^k) lower bound.

A rigorous construction: augment the internal state with a sequence of bits indicating which operations were performed. Since the FV depends on the entire state, different histories yield different FV profiles. As long as the internal state space is sufficiently large to distinguish n^k histories (e.g., a memory register with k slots), the number of distinct FV equivalence classes is at least the number of possible sequences, i.e., n^k. Thus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) = \Omega(n^k). ∎

