Appendix F. Sample Complexity of Meta‑Cognitive Policy Learning

Theorem F.1 (Sample Complexity). Suppose the internal state space \mathcal{S} is finite with |\mathcal{S}| = M, FV values are bounded in [-B,B], and costs are known. For any \epsilon, \delta > 0, there exists an algorithm that, with probability 1-\delta, learns an \epsilon-optimal meta‑policy over all operations in \mathcal{O}_K (depth \le K) using

O\left( \frac{B^2 n^K K M}{\epsilon^2} \log\frac{n^K M}{\delta} \right)

samples from the generative model of internal transitions.

Proof. The meta‑policy chooses at each state the operation (or stop) with highest estimated FV. The total number of operation candidates up to depth K is at most N_{\text{ops}} = n^K (worst‑case enumeration without pruning). For each candidate o and each state s, we need to estimate \mathrm{FV}(o; s) to within \epsilon/2 with high probability. Since executing o from s requires simulating up to K internal steps, each sample costs O(K) transitions. For a given (s, o), we can obtain an independent estimate of Q(s,o) by performing m rollouts: simulate the sequence, then use the world model to estimate V^* at the final state. By Hoeffding’s inequality, m = O( \frac{B^2}{\epsilon^2} \log\frac{1}{\delta'} ) suffices to ensure | \widehat{\mathrm{FV}} - \mathrm{FV} | \le \epsilon/2 with probability at least 1-\delta'. Union‑bounding over all N_{\text{ops}} M state‑operation pairs, set \delta' = \delta/(N_{\text{ops}} M). Total sample complexity: N_{\text{ops}} M \cdot m \cdot K = O( \frac{B^2 n^K K M}{\epsilon^2} \log\frac{n^K M}{\delta} ). ∎

When \mathcal{S} is continuous with finite covering number \mathcal{N}(\epsilon), the same bound holds with M replaced by \mathcal{N}(\epsilon).

