Appendix G. Unification Proofs (Perception, Reasoning, Memory)

Perception. A perception operation o_{\text{perc}} acquires an observation y with encoding cost \lambda I(X;Y|s). The resulting state s' = s \oplus (y, \text{enc}(y)). The FV is

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \mathbb{E}_{y}[V^*(s')] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y|s),

which is exactly the decision‑centric rate‑distortion objective of Maakoul (2026a). The agent perceives iff \mathrm{FV} > 0.

Reasoning. A reasoning step o_{\text{reason}} transforms the internal state via a thought token, with cost c. Its FV reduces to Future Reasoning Value (FRV), and optimal stopping is when \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0.

Memory. Storing a memory m costs c_{\text{store}}; the state is augmented with m. The FMV is  \mathrm{FMV} = \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}} , and the admission rule keeps only positive‑FMV items.

These specializations are immediate from (A.2).

